      description: "Beta-lactams are bactericidal cell-wall drugs whose four-membered beta-lactam ring resembles the terminal D-Ala-D-Ala substrate used by penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs). The drug acylates an active-site serine in a transpeptidase PBP, stopping the cross-links that give peptidoglycan tensile strength. Growing bacteria continue remodeling the wall, autolysins keep cutting old bonds, and the inadequately cross-linked cell eventually lyses under osmotic stress. That shared chemistry does not make the class interchangeable: each molecule has different access through Gram-negative porins, PBP preferences, beta-lactamase stability, tissue penetration, and renal handling. A beta-lactam can therefore fail because the organism is resistant, the concentration spends too little time above the MIC, the drug cannot reach the compartment, the inoculum or device persists, or source control is absent.",
      mechanism: "The pharmacodynamic target is usually the fraction of the dosing interval during which unbound drug exceeds the organism's MIC, written fT>MIC. Once concentrations are sufficiently above the MIC, a much higher peak usually adds less than keeping exposure above the target longer; this explains frequent dosing, continuous or prolonged infusions in selected severe infections, and the danger of missed doses. Resistance can arise before the drug reaches a PBP through porin loss or efflux, at the target through altered PBPs, or by hydrolysis through beta-lactamases. Inhibitors only neutralize the enzyme families they bind, so adding one does not create universal coverage. The clinically useful sequence is identify organism and site, obtain cultures, choose the narrowest reliably active agent, optimize exposure for current renal function, control the source, and de-escalate when data arrive.",
