      description: "Beta-lactamase inhibitors are enzyme-specific shields, not generic antibiotic boosters. Clavulanate, sulbactam, and tazobactam are beta-lactam-based suicide inhibitors with strongest activity against many class A enzymes but unreliable activity against AmpC, most carbapenemases, and many inhibitor-resistant enzymes. Avibactam is a reversible diazabicyclooctane inhibitor active against many class A enzymes including KPC, class C AmpC, and some class D OXA-48-like enzymes, but not metallo-beta-lactamases. Relebactam has strong class A/C activity but not OXA-48 or MBL activity. Vaborbactam is a cyclic boronate designed especially for KPC and other class A/C serine enzymes, not OXA-48 or MBLs. Durlobactam protects sulbactam against broad class A, C, and D Acinetobacter enzymes. Enmetazobactam primarily extends cefepime against ESBL-producing Enterobacterales.",
      mechanism: "Classical inhibitors resemble beta-lactams and form slowly reversible or irreversible covalent complexes with susceptible serine beta-lactamases. Diazabicyclooctanes acylate and can recyclize from the active-site serine, allowing repeated enzyme inhibition. Boronates mimic the tetrahedral transition state of beta-lactam hydrolysis. Metallo-beta-lactamases such as NDM, VIM, and IMP use zinc-activated water rather than an active-site serine, so these serine-directed inhibitors fail. Aztreonam is intrinsically stable to MBL hydrolysis but can be destroyed by co-produced ESBL, AmpC, KPC, or OXA enzymes; pairing aztreonam with avibactam protection can therefore create a mechanistic solution for selected MBL-producing Enterobacterales.",
