      adverseEffects: ["Most clinical adverse effects come from the complete combination, including allergy, diarrhea/CDI, hepatic injury, renal accumulation, cytopenias, and partner-specific neurotoxicity.", "Clavulanate is strongly associated with cholestatic or mixed liver injury; sulbactam uniquely has direct PBP activity against susceptible Acinetobacter."],
      contraindications: ["Do not use avibactam, relebactam, or vaborbactam as though they directly kill bacteria because these inhibitors do not provide dependable standalone bactericidal activity; the active beta-lactam partner must still reach and bind susceptible PBPs.", "Do not expect current serine beta-lactamase inhibitors to inhibit NDM, VIM, or IMP because those enzymes use zinc chemistry rather than an acylatable serine."],
      nursingEssentials: ["When a report names ESBL, AmpC, KPC, OXA-48-like, NDM, VIM, IMP, or OXA-type Acinetobacter enzymes, connect that name to the inhibitor spectrum and confirm the final susceptibility rather than stopping at 'carbapenem resistant.'", "Monitor the organ clearance and toxicity of both components because the inhibitor and partner may require coordinated renal adjustment to preserve their exposure ratio."],
      interactions: ["Combination products are fixed ratios or co-packaged regimens; substituting components or doses can destroy the exposure relationship that was studied.", "The partner beta-lactam carries its own interactions, including carbapenem-valproate depletion and cefepime-related neurotoxicity when renal clearance falls."],
      keyLabs: ["Species, MIC, phenotypic carbapenemase result, molecular resistance gene when available, creatinine/eGFR, CBC, liver tests, and clinical/source response.", "A resistance gene predicts enzyme capability, while the measured MIC integrates that enzyme with expression, permeability, efflux, and target changes; both levels of information matter."],
      nclexTraps: ["Avibactam covers KPC and some OXA-48-like enzymes but not metallo-beta-lactamases.", "Sulbactam is more than a shield in Acinetobacter because it can directly bind PBP1 and PBP3."],
