      description: "ESBLs are usually class A enzymes such as CTX-M that hydrolyze extended-spectrum penicillins and cephalosporins but are often inhibited in vitro by classical inhibitors; serious nonurinary ESBL infections commonly favor a carbapenem because clinical reliability matters beyond an in-vitro inhibitor effect. AmpC enzymes are class C cephalosporinases that can be constitutive, plasmid-mediated, or inducible; organisms such as Enterobacter cloacae complex, Klebsiella aerogenes, and Citrobacter freundii can derepress production during therapy, undermining drugs that look initially susceptible. KPC is a class A carbapenemase inhibited by avibactam, vaborbactam, and relebactam. OXA-48-like enzymes are class D carbapenemases with variable cephalosporin hydrolysis; avibactam can inhibit many, while vaborbactam and relebactam are not reliable solutions. NDM, VIM, and IMP are class B metallo-beta-lactamases that use zinc and escape current serine inhibitors.",
      mechanism: "A resistance phenotype is the sum of hydrolysis rate, enzyme amount, drug entry, efflux, PBP affinity, and inoculum. AmpC induction matters because exposure to selected beta-lactams activates cell-wall recycling signals that increase ampC transcription; a growing enzyme concentration can raise the MIC during treatment. Carbapenemase names matter because inhibitor chemistry is specific. MBLs cannot hydrolyze aztreonam efficiently, but the same organism often carries an ESBL, AmpC, KPC, or OXA enzyme that destroys aztreonam. Avibactam can inhibit those co-enzymes while aztreonam bypasses the MBL, explaining the paired strategy for selected MBL-producing Enterobacterales.",
