      description: "Pseudomonas is difficult because its outer membrane admits drugs poorly, OprD loss can block carbapenem entry, Mex efflux pumps remove drug, chromosomal Pseudomonas-derived cephalosporinase (AmpC/PDC) hydrolyzes beta-lactams, and PBP mutations can reduce target affinity. Traditional active beta-lactams include piperacillin/tazobactam, ceftazidime, cefepime, aztreonam, imipenem/cilastatin, and meropenem when the isolate is susceptible. Ertapenem is not dependable. Ceftolozane has unusually strong intrinsic antipseudomonal PBP activity and stability against several non-carbapenemase mechanisms; ceftazidime/avibactam can overcome selected AmpC and serine beta-lactamases; imipenem/relebactam can restore activity when class A/C enzymes and permeability contribute; cefiderocol exploits iron transport. None is universally active.",
      mechanism: "The susceptibility result integrates several barriers at once. A carbapenem-resistant isolate may remain cefepime- or piperacillin/tazobactam-susceptible when OprD loss selectively impairs carbapenem entry, while combined AmpC upregulation, efflux, and PBP changes can defeat multiple classes without a named carbapenemase. Extended infusion can improve fT>MIC for a susceptible MIC but cannot rescue true resistance. Newer drugs should be matched to the likely mechanism: ceftolozane/tazobactam is often valuable for non-carbapenemase DTR Pseudomonas, while ceftazidime/avibactam or imipenem/relebactam fits selected serine-enzyme profiles. MBL-producing Pseudomonas remains especially difficult and requires specialist interpretation.",
