      description: "Extended-infusion beta-lactam therapy is an antibiotic dosing strategy that increases the fraction of each dosing interval during which unbound drug remains above the organism's MIC by delivering the same or protocol-specified dose over a longer period. This works because beta-lactams are primarily time-dependent killers: after the concentration is sufficiently above the MIC, a towering peak contributes less than sustained PBP occupancy. A three- or four-hour infusion can therefore improve target attainment when the MIC is near the susceptibility breakpoint, volume of distribution is expanded, renal clearance is brisk, or the infection is severe. It is not a stronger drug and cannot reverse an MIC that reflects true resistance, absent source control, or a compartment the molecule cannot enter.",
      mechanism: "A short infusion produces a high peak followed by decline; a prolonged infusion lowers that peak but lengthens fT>MIC. Continuous infusion can maintain an even concentration after an appropriate loading dose. The loading dose matters because beginning slowly without first filling the expanded central volume can delay therapeutic exposure in sepsis. Renal function changes the slope: augmented renal clearance shortens time above MIC and may require greater total exposure, while kidney failure prolongs exposure and can cause neurotoxicity. Stability after preparation, line access, compatibility, nursing workflow, and interruption time determine whether the theoretical pharmacokinetic advantage reaches the patient.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Do not extend an infusion by improvisation. Product stability, concentration, diluent, line compatibility, loading dose, total daily dose, renal adjustment, and protocol must all support the regimen."),
