      contraindications: ["Do not use extended infusion as a substitute for changing an inactive antibiotic because more time above an MIC cannot overcome a resistance mechanism that makes achievable concentrations ineffective.", "Do not omit a loading dose in a critically ill patient solely because maintenance infusion is prolonged; loading addresses volume, while maintenance addresses clearance."],
      nursingEssentials: ["Verify exact start and stop time, loading dose, pump rate, concentration, stability window, lumen compatibility, renal function, and missed-time plan because a nominal four-hour order that repeatedly pauses may deliver worse exposure than intended.", "Escalate rapid renal change, unexpected neurologic findings, or repeated line conflicts so pharmacy and the clinical team can recalculate rather than silently preserving an obsolete regimen."],
      interactions: ["Line compatibility becomes a pharmacologic issue because sharing a lumen can interrupt exposure or precipitate drugs; use validated compatibility data and dedicated access when required.", "Renal replacement therapy, extracorporeal circuits, burns, obesity, edema, and augmented clearance can alter exposure enough to justify protocol-specific dosing or TDM."],
      keyLabs: ["Culture/MIC, serial creatinine and urine output, renal-replacement settings, infusion record, and beta-lactam level where a validated TDM program exists.", "The MIC is not a measured patient concentration; target attainment compares the organism's MIC with the patient's unbound concentration over time."],
      nclexTraps: ["Extended infusion improves time above MIC; it does not improve therapy by producing a higher peak.", "A longer infusion is an exposure strategy, not evidence that the organism is susceptible."],
