      description: "Beta-lactam allergy is not one diagnosis. Immediate IgE-mediated reactions usually occur within minutes to hours and can produce urticaria, angioedema, bronchospasm, hypotension, or anaphylaxis. Delayed T-cell syndromes include benign maculopapular eruption as well as dangerous DRESS, SJS/TEN, AGEP, hepatitis, nephritis, or cytopenias. Nausea, diarrhea, headache, family history, or an unknown childhood event is not equivalent to anaphylaxis. Cross-reactivity is driven more by similar R1 side chains than by the beta-lactam ring alone; cefazolin has a distinctive side chain, while amoxicillin/ampicillin share similarities with selected first-generation oral cephalosporins. Aztreonam has minimal penicillin cross-reactivity but shares a side chain with ceftazidime.",
