      usedToTreat: "A bedside framework for starting enough antibiotic soon enough in severe infection without allowing broad empiric therapy to become indefinite unexamined therapy.",
      description: "Empiric antibiotic therapy uses the suspected source, illness severity, host risks, prior microbiology, local resistance, allergy, and organ function to select an initially active regimen before the organism is fully known; that probability-based choice is deliberately time limited. Blood and source cultures should be obtained first when this does not dangerously delay treatment because antibiotics can sterilize specimens and erase the evidence needed to narrow. In septic shock, prompt active therapy matters, so sampling and administration must happen efficiently rather than sequentially for convenience. Once microbiology and clinical data arrive, the regimen should become narrower, stop duplicate coverage, target the confirmed organism, and acquire a defined duration anchor.",
