      aliases: ["meningitis beta lactams", "ceftriaxone versus cefotaxime newborn", "Listeria meningitis ampicillin", "meningitis cephalosporin coverage", "why no ceftriaxone neonate", "meropenem meningitis"],
      class: "Beta-lactam meningitis and neonatal pharmacotherapy pathway",
      classExampleNames: ["Ampicillin", "Ceftriaxone", "Cefotaxime", "Meropenem", "Penicillin G"],
      classExampleKeys: ["ampicillin", "ceftriaxone", "cefotaxime", "meropenem", "penicillin g"],
      classPathway: ["Age and host predict organisms", "Inflamed meninges alter penetration", "Agent-specific neonatal toxicity changes the regimen"],
      usedToTreat: "Bacterial meningitis and neonatal serious infection with age-appropriate therapy that covers likely organisms and avoids preventable ceftriaxone-related bilirubin or calcium harm.",
      description: "Meningitis requires bactericidal exposure in cerebrospinal fluid, not merely serum activity. Inflamed meninges increase penetration of selected beta-lactams, but protein binding, molecular properties, dose, and organism MIC still matter. Ceftriaxone or cefotaxime is central for many community bacterial meningitis regimens because each reaches inflamed CSF and covers susceptible pneumococcus, meningococcus, and Haemophilus. Neither covers Listeria monocytogenes, so ampicillin is added when age, pregnancy, immune suppression, or other risk makes Listeria plausible. Neonates have a different pathogen set that includes group B Streptococcus, E. coli and other Gram-negatives, and Listeria; cefotaxime has historically been used when a third-generation cephalosporin is needed because ceftriaxone can displace bilirubin and precipitate with IV calcium.",
      mechanism: "Ceftriaxone is highly protein bound and can compete with bilirubin for albumin binding. Neonates have lower binding reserve and an immature blood-brain barrier, so increased free bilirubin can enter brain tissue and cause kernicterus. Ceftriaxone can also form insoluble calcium salts in neonatal lungs and kidneys, explaining strict age- and calcium-related restrictions. Ampicillin reaches CSF better when meninges are inflamed and binds susceptible Listeria and enterococcal PBPs. Meropenem can reach CSF and is labeled for bacterial meningitis in selected age groups, but spectrum, resistance, seizure risk, renal dose, and stewardship determine when its breadth is justified.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Do not give ceftriaxone to a hyperbilirubinemic neonate or ignore neonatal IV calcium restrictions. This is a precipitation and bilirubin-neurotoxicity problem, not a minor formulation preference."),
      adverseEffects: ["Neurologic deterioration can reflect infection, pressure, stroke, seizure, immune inflammation, inadequate exposure, resistance, or drug neurotoxicity; reassess rather than attributing every change to meningitis alone.", "High-dose prolonged beta-lactam therapy can cause cytopenias, hepatic or renal injury, CDI, allergy, and concentration-related seizures."],
      contraindications: ["Do not use ceftriaxone or cefotaxime alone when Listeria coverage is required because third-generation cephalosporins lack dependable activity.", "Do not use routine-dose non-CNS regimens for meningitis because serum susceptibility does not guarantee adequate CSF exposure."],
      nursingEssentials: ["Give time-critical empiric therapy after blood cultures and lumbar puncture when safe, but do not delay antibiotics for imaging or LP in an unstable patient. Document exact first-dose time because neurologic outcome depends on timely active exposure.", "Track mental status, pupils, focal findings, seizures, hemodynamics, sodium, cultures, CSF indices, hearing risk, renal function, and steroid timing because management extends beyond selecting an antibiotic."],
      interactions: ["Ceftriaxone-calcium restrictions are age and route specific; use the current label and neonatal policy rather than relying on a vague memory that calcium is always or never allowed.", "Carbapenems lower valproate, which is especially dangerous in a neurologically vulnerable patient with seizures."],
      keyLabs: ["Blood and CSF culture, Gram stain, cell count/differential, protein, glucose paired with serum glucose, PCR when indicated, CBC, creatinine/eGFR, bilirubin in neonates, and organism MIC.", "A negative culture after antibiotics does not exclude bacterial meningitis; interpret CSF physiology, PCR, pretreatment timing, and syndrome."],
