      description: "Sulbactam is unusual among older beta-lactamase inhibitors because it directly kills susceptible Acinetobacter by binding PBP1 and PBP3, enzymes required for cell-wall synthesis. In ampicillin/sulbactam, the ampicillin treats susceptible partner organisms while sulbactam contributes the clinically important Acinetobacter activity. Carbapenem-resistant A. baumannii often produces OXA-type and other serine beta-lactamases that destroy sulbactam. Durlobactam was designed to inhibit broad class A, C, and D Acinetobacter enzymes and protect sulbactam long enough to reach its PBP targets. The Xacduro product is therefore not two passive inhibitors: sulbactam is the antibacterial engine and durlobactam is its enzyme shield.",
      mechanism: "Sulbactam acylates Acinetobacter PBP1 and PBP3, disrupting elongation and septation. Durlobactam is a diazabicyclooctane that reversibly acylates susceptible serine beta-lactamases, including many ADC class C and OXA class D enzymes. It does not inhibit class B metallo-beta-lactamases and has no useful antibacterial activity alone. Severe CRAB infection also reflects biofilm, devices, ventilator exposure, tissue necrosis, and limited host reserve, so current guidance commonly uses a sulbactam-containing backbone with a second active agent in important disease rather than treating one MIC as the whole problem.",
