      adverseEffects: ["Liver-test abnormalities, diarrhea, anemia, hypokalemia, hypersensitivity, CDI, and infusion-related complications can occur with sulbactam/durlobactam.", "Treatment failure can reflect an MBL, altered PBPs, permeability/efflux, high burden, abscess or device source, or inadequate partner strategy rather than absence of durlobactam exposure alone."],
      contraindications: ["Serious hypersensitivity to sulbactam, durlobactam, or other beta-lactams is a contraindication to Xacduro under the current label.", "Do not interpret the word inhibitor as meaning sulbactam lacks direct antibacterial activity against Acinetobacter; that misconception can lead to poor regimen design."],
      nursingEssentials: ["Verify the organism is Acinetobacter baumannii-calcoaceticus complex, review susceptibility and resistance mechanism, assess pneumonia and ventilator source, and coordinate renal-adjusted coadministration because both components must arrive in the studied relationship.", "Trend oxygenation, hemodynamics, respiratory cultures, source control, creatinine, liver tests, CBC, and potassium because response and toxicity are multi-organ signals."],
      interactions: ["Xacduro is co-packaged and preparation is product specific; do not substitute arbitrary separate-component doses.", "Concurrent nephrotoxic salvage agents can narrow the safety margin and indirectly alter sulbactam/durlobactam clearance."],
      keyLabs: ["Culture/MIC, carbapenemase or resistance testing where available, creatinine/eGFR, liver tests, CBC, potassium, oxygenation, and pneumonia imaging or source assessment.", "For sulbactam, fT>MIC predicts activity; for durlobactam, exposure relative to the combination MIC predicts protection, so coordinated dosing matters."],
      nclexTraps: ["Sulbactam directly binds Acinetobacter PBPs; durlobactam protects it from serine beta-lactamases.", "Durlobactam alone is not an antibiotic and does not inhibit metallo-beta-lactamases."],
